description: An open framework for AI-native companies. zeno is an always-on orchestrator that runs your company's processes and puts agents to work in one space your whole team shares, on infrastructure you own.
---
<section class="lp-hero">
  <div class="lp-hero-bg" aria-hidden="true"></div>
  <div class="lp-container lp-hero-inner">
    <span class="lp-eyebrow">Open source · Self-hosted</span>
    <h1 class="lp-title">An open framework for<br /><span class="lp-grad">AI-native companies</span></h1>
    <p class="lp-sub">
      Meet <strong>zeno</strong> — an always-on orchestrator that runs your
      company's processes and puts agents to work in one space your whole team
      shares, on infrastructure you own. Describe the work once; it gets done.
    </p>
    <div class="lp-cta-row">
      <a class="lp-btn lp-btn-primary" href="/docs/">Read the docs</a>
      <a class="lp-btn lp-btn-ghost" href="{{ site.github }}" rel="noopener">GitHub ↗</a>
    </div>
    <ul class="lp-chips">
      <li>One shared space</li>
      <li>Deterministic by default</li>
      <li>Cheaper the more it runs</li>
      <li>Self-hosted</li>
      <li>Open source</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="lp-lede">
  <div class="lp-container">
    <p>
      An AI-native company runs on agents. zeno makes them
      <strong>trustworthy</strong> — working in the open where your team can see
      them — and <strong>cheaper the more they run</strong>, on infrastructure
      you own.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="lp-section">
  <div class="lp-container">
    <h2 class="lp-h2">A team can't run on personal agents</h2>
    <p class="lp-section-lead">
      Today's AI agents are personal, per-tab, and forgetful. That's fine for
      one person — it breaks the moment a team tries to run real work on it.
    </p>
    <div class="lp-shift-grid">
      <div class="lp-shift-card lp-shift-old">
        <div class="lp-shift-label">The old way</div>
        <p>
          Everyone drives their own agent in their own tab, then merges the
          results by hand. Work diverges, nothing carries over from one task to
          the next, security is bolted on afterward, and the whole thing lives
          on someone else's platform.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="lp-shift-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</div>
      <div class="lp-shift-card lp-shift-new">
        <div class="lp-shift-label">With zeno</div>
        <p>
          One orchestrator runs your processes in a space the whole team shares.
          Agents work in that same context — reading the same discussion,
          writing to the same knowledge base — on infrastructure you own. Team
          agents, not personal ones. Nothing to merge.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="lp-section">
  <div class="lp-container">
    <h2 class="lp-h2">One orchestrator at the center</h2>
    <p class="lp-section-lead">
      zeno is the always-on core. It runs your company's processes and calls in
      an agent only for the parts that need judgement — each one given just the
      access its task requires, and nothing more.
    </p>
    <div class="lp-stack">
      <div class="lp-tier lp-tier-comms">
        <div class="lp-tier-name">The shared space</div>
        <div class="lp-tier-desc">
          Your team and its agents in one place, over the channels you already
          use — chat, Telegram, GitHub issues, email.
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="lp-conn">↕ messages in · answers out</div>
      <div class="lp-tier lp-tier-zeno">
        <div class="lp-tier-name"><span>zeno</span> — the orchestrator</div>
        <div class="lp-tier-desc">
          Runs your processes, spawns short-lived, scoped agents for the
          judgement calls, and asks the team when it needs a decision — picking
          up right where it left off when the reply lands.
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="lp-conn">↑ helped by</div>
      <div class="lp-tier">
        <div class="lp-tier-name">What helps you run it</div>
        <div class="lp-tier-desc">
          <strong>nixops</strong> stands zeno up on servers you own.
          <strong>Machines</strong> are ready-made workflows — like publishing
          or research — you drop in to fill it with work.
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="lp-section">
  <div class="lp-container">
    <h2 class="lp-h2">What it runs today</h2>
    <p class="lp-section-lead">
      Not a mockup. Here's a real setup running now — a small team's publishing
      pipeline, run end to end by agents they talk to like teammates.
    </p>
    <div class="lp-stack">
      <div class="lp-tier lp-tier-comms">
        <div class="lp-tier-name">1 · You write a note</div>
        <div class="lp-tier-desc">
          Someone drops a short note in the team's chat channel.
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="lp-conn">↓</div>
      <div class="lp-tier lp-tier-zeno">
        <div class="lp-tier-name">2 · Agents publish it</div>
        <div class="lp-tier-desc">
          They translate it, publish it to the team's website and its broadcast
          channel, and reply the links right back into the chat thread.
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="lp-conn">↓</div>
      <div class="lp-tier">
        <div class="lp-tier-name">3 · And research it</div>
        <div class="lp-tier-desc">
          Another agent reads the new post and files what it learned into the
          team's linked knowledge wiki.
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="lp-section-lead">
      Every one of them is a bot the team chats with, running on their own
      servers. If a step fails halfway, the next run picks up where it left off
      instead of doing the work twice.
      <a href="/docs/zeno/">See how it works →</a>
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="lp-section">
  <div class="lp-container">
    <h2 class="lp-h2">It gets cheaper the more it runs</h2>
    <p class="lp-section-lead">
      Most AI agents are goldfish — they solve every task from scratch and
      forget it the moment it's done.
    </p>
    <div class="lp-shift-grid">
      <div class="lp-shift-card lp-shift-old">
        <div class="lp-shift-label">Goldfish</div>
        <p>
          Do the same job a thousand times and you pay full price a thousand
          times. Nothing carries over. The cost never comes down.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="lp-shift-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</div>
      <div class="lp-shift-card lp-shift-new">
        <div class="lp-shift-label">A company that remembers</div>
        <p>
          zeno turns work it has already figured out into reusable process. The
          first run is the expensive one; after that, repeats run on their own —
          cheaper and faster every time. Experience becomes an asset that grows
          with use.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="lp-section-lead">
      Cost-per-task falling as volume grows is the bet we're building toward —
      proven out one working workflow at a time, not a number we're quoting.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="lp-section">
  <div class="lp-container">
    <h2 class="lp-h2">Why it's different</h2>
    <p class="lp-section-lead">Three things most agent tools don't give a team at once.</p>
    <div class="lp-principle-grid">
      <div class="lp-principle">
        <h4>Not a personal tab</h4>
        <p>Personal agent apps give each person a private assistant. zeno is one shared space for the whole team, so work doesn't diverge and there's nothing to merge by hand.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="lp-principle">
        <h4>Not per-task glue</h4>
        <p>Other tools wire up each workflow by hand, every time. zeno's processes are deterministic and compounding, and every agent is scoped to just what its task needs.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="lp-principle">
        <h4>Not a closed platform</h4>
        <p>No service in the middle. zeno is open source and self-hosted — your processes, your data, your servers.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="lp-section">
  <div class="lp-container">
    <h2 class="lp-h2">The open-source pieces</h2>
    <p class="lp-section-lead">zeno is the product. The rest of the stack exists to make it easy to run. Start with zeno; add the others when you need them.</p>
    <div class="lp-pillar-grid">
      {% for project in site.projects %}
        <article class="lp-pillar">
          <div class="lp-pillar-icon">
            {% if project.slug == "zeno" %}
              <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="3" /><circle cx="12" cy="3.6" r="1.6" /><circle cx="4.3" cy="18" r="1.6" /><circle cx="19.7" cy="18" r="1.6" /><path d="M12 9V5.2M10.2 13.7 6 16.6M13.8 13.7 18 16.6" /></svg>
            {% elif project.slug == "nixops" %}
              <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><rect x="3.5" y="4" width="17" height="6" rx="1.5" /><rect x="3.5" y="14" width="17" height="6" rx="1.5" /><path d="M7 7h.01M7 17h.01" /></svg>
            {% else %}
              <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="8" /></svg>
            {% endif %}
          </div>
          <div class="lp-pillar-role">{{ project.role }}</div>
          <h3>{{ project.name }}</h3>
          <p>{{ project.tagline }}</p>
          <div class="lp-pillar-links">
            {% if project.docs %}<a href="{{ project.docs }}">Docs →</a>{% endif %}
            <a href="{{ project.repo }}" rel="noopener">GitHub ↗</a>
          </div>
        </article>
      {% endfor %}
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="lp-section">
  <div class="lp-container">
    <h2 class="lp-h2">Principles</h2>
    <p class="lp-section-lead">The beliefs the whole stack is built on.</p>
    <div class="lp-principle-grid">
      <div class="lp-principle">
        <h4>One shared context</h4>
        <p>Work happens in a space the whole team sees, not in private tabs. Agents read and write the same context, so there is nothing to merge.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="lp-principle">
        <h4>Deterministic by default</h4>
        <p>Spell out the routine once and it runs the same way every time. An agent is called in only where something genuinely needs judgement — not for everything.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="lp-principle">
        <h4>Security by design</h4>
        <p>Security is built into the structure, not bolted on. Each agent gets only the access its task needs and nothing more — secrets stay with the orchestrator, so a stray instruction can't reach what was never handed over.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="lp-principle">
        <h4>Meet humans where they are</h4>
        <p>Communication is separated from work. An agent asks the team over whatever channel you already use — chat, Telegram, GitHub issues, email — and resumes when the answer lands.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="lp-principle">
        <h4>Own your stack</h4>
        <p>Self-hosted from the metal up. Your processes, your data, your servers — no platform in the middle.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="lp-principle">
        <h4>Reuse, don't reinvent</h4>
        <p>Best-in-class harness, mature communication software, proven infrastructure. We build the connective tissue and the boundaries between them — not worse copies of what already works.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="lp-cta-band">
  <div class="lp-container lp-cta-inner">
    <h2>Build a company that runs itself.</h2>
    <p>Start with the orchestrator and grow the stack from there.</p>
    <div class="lp-cta-row">
      <a class="lp-btn lp-btn-primary" href="/docs/zeno/">Start with zeno</a>
      <a class="lp-btn lp-btn-ghost" href="{{ site.github }}" rel="noopener">GitHub ↗</a>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
